<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  /etc/fpms/fastdds_udp_only.xml - force Fast DDS onto UDPv4 only.

  This is the highest-value file in FPMS-OS. Read docs/DDS.md before changing it.

  THE BUG
  =======
  Fast DDS enables a shared-memory transport by default and prefers it for
  same-host communication. On this hardware, SHM segments do not reliably
  survive across what amount to separate "process eras" - a publisher started
  in one era and a subscriber started in another complete DISCOVERY correctly
  and then never exchange a single byte.

  The repository documents this twice, in two different subsystems, without
  ever naming the cause:

    units/fpms-ros-settle.service
      "the node is active, it logs `scans=2848 dropped=0 rate=9.55 Hz`, and
       `ros2 topic info /scan_lidar` reports 'Publisher count: 1'. And yet NO
       subscriber created afterwards ever receives a single LaserScan...
       Restarting the unit fixes it instantly and reproducibly."

    ROS_PORT.md  (root cause section, reproduced three times)
      "rosbridge only delivers topics whose PUBLISHER already existed when
       rosbridge started. A publisher created afterwards is never discovered,
       and the client subscription asking for it is accepted and then silent
       forever... connected, subscribed, silent - no error anywhere."

      Evidence: after a reboot, fpms-rosbridge started at 22:26:33 and
      fpms-odom-tf at 22:45:45 - nineteen minutes later - and /odom delivered
      0 messages while publishing healthily at 6.6 Hz. A restart gave 53
      messages in 8 seconds.

  Those are the same bug. "The publisher must pre-exist the subscriber" is a
  description of the symptom, not a cause. The cause is the transport.

  WHAT THE MITIGATIONS COST TODAY
  ===============================
    - fpms-ros-settle.service sleeps 75 seconds after boot and then blindly
      restarts fpms-lidar-ros and fpms-tf. That is ~2 minutes of boot-to-usable
      spent on this.
    - deploy_stack.sh must restart the four ROS consumers in a block AFTER
      every publisher, and documents why at length.
    - fpms-uros-supervisor incidentally re-creates participants and papers over
      it further.

  None of those are fixes. This file is.

  HOW TO VERIFY IT WORKED
  =======================
  The check must exercise the case that fails today: a subscriber created LONG
  after the publisher. A topic list or `ros2 topic info` will pass either way -
  discovery was never the broken part.

      # leave the stack running for a few minutes, then, in a fresh shell:
      export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=20
      export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_fastrtps_cpp
      export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/etc/fpms/fastdds_udp_only.xml
      ros2 topic hz /scan_lidar        # must report a rate, not hang

  `fpms-selftest` performs exactly this check on every boot.

  A NOTE ON ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY
  ============================
  Every FPMS unit also sets ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY=1. That is complementary, not
  redundant: localhost-only restricts which interfaces DDS uses, but it does
  NOT disable the shared-memory transport. You need both. Localhost-only is
  safe here because every ROS node runs on the Pi - the operator laptop reaches
  the rover over the Foxglove/rosbridge websockets and MQTT, and neither of
  those uses DDS.

  IF THIS FILE IS MALFORMED IT IS SILENTLY IGNORED
  ================================================
  Fast DDS does not fail loudly on a profile it cannot parse - it logs at a
  level nobody reads and falls back to defaults, which reintroduces exactly the
  bug this file exists to remove. scripts/40-ros-layer.sh therefore parses this
  file at build time and FAILS THE BUILD if it does not, and fpms-selftest
  re-checks it at boot.

  SCHEMA
  ======
  This is the Fast DDS profile schema as shipped with ROS 2 Humble
  (Fast DDS 2.6.x). Two things commonly get this wrong:
    - the namespace must be the fastRTPS_Profiles one below, not the newer
      eProsima "fastdds" namespace used by 2.10+;
    - <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports> is what actually
      removes SHM. Listing a UDPv4 transport in <userTransports> alone is not
      enough - the builtin set, which includes SHM, is still added alongside it
      and Fast DDS will still prefer SHM for same-host peers.
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <profiles>

    <transport_descriptors>
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>fpms_udpv4</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>

        <!-- Large enough for a full LaserScan and the camera-adjacent traffic
             without fragmenting on the loopback path. 64 KiB is the practical
             UDP datagram ceiling; the socket buffers are sized to match so a
             burst of scans during a costmap rebuild is not dropped at the
             kernel. -->
        <maxMessageSize>65500</maxMessageSize>
        <sendBufferSize>1048576</sendBufferSize>
        <receiveBufferSize>4194304</receiveBufferSize>
      </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="fpms_udp_only_participant" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>fpms_udpv4</transport_id>
        </userTransports>

        <!-- THE LOAD-BEARING LINE. Without this, the builtin transports
             (which include shared memory) are added in addition to ours and
             SHM is still preferred for same-host communication - meaning the
             bug is entirely unfixed while the profile looks correct. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>

        <builtin>
          <!-- Everything on this robot is on this robot. Keeping discovery
               traffic off the WiFi also removes a second, independent source
               of flakiness: the link has been measured at ~108 ms RTT on the
               operator hotspot where 2-5 ms is expected, and swings between
               -47 and -78 dBm. Below about -70 dBm the LiDAR telemetry stops.
               DDS discovery does not belong on that link. -->
          <metatrafficUnicastLocatorList>
            <locator>
              <udpv4>
                <address>127.0.0.1</address>
              </udpv4>
            </locator>
          </metatrafficUnicastLocatorList>

          <initialPeersList>
            <locator>
              <udpv4>
                <address>127.0.0.1</address>
              </udpv4>
            </locator>
          </initialPeersList>

          <!-- Announce often enough that a node starting late is found
               quickly. The default 3s lease means a consumer restarted after
               a publisher can wait noticeably long before traffic flows, and
               "wait and see" is precisely the diagnostic state this project
               keeps getting stuck in. -->
          <discovery_config>
            <leaseDuration>
              <sec>10</sec>
            </leaseDuration>
            <leaseAnnouncement>
              <sec>3</sec>
            </leaseAnnouncement>
          </discovery_config>
        </builtin>
      </rtps>
    </participant>

  </profiles>
</dds>
